Absolutely. These are senior/techno-managerial interview topics, so I’ll explain them from three angles: concept → internal working → production/interview problem-solving, with Java/Spring Boot examples where relevant.

1. Microservices + Fallback Mechanisms

Microservices split a large application into independently deployable services.

Example:

                    API Gateway
                         |
          +--------------+--------------+
          |              |              |
     Order Service   Payment Service  Inventory Service
          |              |              |
        MySQL          Payment API      Redis/DB

The biggest problem isn't creating microservices. It is handling failure between services.

Suppose:

Order → Payment → Bank API

If Bank API takes 30 seconds, Payment becomes slow, then Order becomes slow, and eventually all request threads can get exhausted.

This is called a cascading failure.

Resilience mechanisms

Typical production stack:

Timeout
   ↓
Retry
   ↓
Circuit Breaker
   ↓
Bulkhead
   ↓
Rate Limiter
   ↓
Fallback

Timeout

Never allow an external call to wait indefinitely.

java
HttpClient timeout = 2 seconds;

Example:

Payment service
     |
     | timeout = 2 sec
     ↓
Bank API

If bank doesn't respond within 2 seconds:

Payment → TIMEOUT

instead of:

Payment → waiting → waiting → waiting → thread exhausted


Retry

Useful for transient failures.

For example:

Request 1 → timeout
Request 2 → success

But don't blindly retry.

Bad:

POST payment
POST payment
POST payment

You could charge the customer three times.

Use retries mainly for:

- network failures
- temporary 5xx
- connection failures

And preferably use idempotency keys for business operations.


Circuit Breaker

Circuit breaker protects your system from an unhealthy dependency.

States:

             failures exceed threshold
 CLOSED ----------------------------> OPEN
   ↑                                  |
   |                                  |
   | successful test                  |
   |                                  ↓
   +---------------------------- HALF_OPEN

CLOSED

Normal operation.

Request → Payment
Request → Payment
Request → Payment

Failures are counted.

Suppose:

failure rate > 50%

Circuit opens.

OPEN

Requests don't even go to Payment.

Order
  |
  X
Payment

Instead:

Order → fallback()

This prevents unnecessary load.

HALF_OPEN

After a configured wait period:

OPEN
 ↓
HALF_OPEN
 ↓
send a few test requests

If successful:

HALF_OPEN → CLOSED

If failures continue:

HALF_OPEN → OPEN


Resilience4j

For Spring Boot, Resilience4j is commonly used.

Typical dependency:

xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>io.github.resilience4j</groupId>
    <artifactId>resilience4j-spring-boot3</artifactId>
</dependency>

Example:

java
@CircuitBreaker(
        name = "paymentService",
        fallbackMethod = "paymentFallback"
)
public PaymentResponse makePayment(PaymentRequest request) {

    return paymentClient.pay(request);
}

Fallback:

java
public PaymentResponse paymentFallback(
        PaymentRequest request,
        Throwable throwable) {

    return PaymentResponse.pending(
            "Payment service temporarily unavailable"
    );
}

Configuration:

yaml
resilience4j:
  circuitbreaker:
    instances:
      paymentService:
        slidingWindowSize: 20
        minimumNumberOfCalls: 10
        failureRateThreshold: 50
        waitDurationInOpenState: 10s
        permittedNumberOfCallsInHalfOpenState: 3


Bulkhead

Bulkhead isolates resources.

Suppose your application has 100 threads.

Without isolation:

Payment calls → 80 threads
Order DB     → 10 threads
Other APIs   → 10 threads

Payment failure can consume all threads.

With bulkhead:

Payment → max 20 threads
Inventory → max 20
Other → remaining

Payment can't destroy the whole application.


Rate Limiter

Protects service from excessive requests.

Example:

100 requests/sec

If client sends:

500 requests/sec

only allowed requests proceed.

Common implementation:

API Gateway
    ↓
Redis Rate Limiter
    ↓
Microservice


