Example:

java
class NumberPrinter {

    private int number = 1;
    private final int limit = 10;

    public synchronized void printOdd()
            throws InterruptedException {

        while (number <= limit) {

            while (number % 2 == 0) {
                wait();
            }

            System.out.println(number++);
            notify();
        }
    }

    public synchronized void printEven()
            throws InterruptedException {

        while (number <= limit) {

            while (number % 2 != 0) {
                wait();
            }

            System.out.println(number++);
            notify();
        }
    }
}

 
5. HashMap Internal Working

This is a must-know Java interview topic.

HashMap internally uses:

array of buckets
+
linked list / tree

Conceptually:

HashMap
   |
   +-- bucket 0
   +-- bucket 1
   +-- bucket 2
   +-- bucket 3

When:

java
map.put("Ankit", 100);

Java calculates:

hashCode()
     ↓
hash transformation
     ↓
bucket index

Conceptually:

index = hash & (n - 1)

where `n` is table size.


Collision

Suppose:

key A → bucket 5
key B → bucket 5

Collision.

Historically:

bucket 5
   ↓
Node A → Node B → Node C

In modern Java HashMap, when a bucket becomes sufficiently collision-heavy, the bucket can be converted into a red-black tree, improving lookup in that bucket from roughly O(n) to O(log n).


HashMap put

Conceptually:

put(key,value)
    ↓
calculate hash
    ↓
calculate bucket
    ↓
bucket empty?
   /      \
 yes       no
 ↓          ↓
insert    compare keys
             ↓
          collision
             ↓
      list/tree insertion


Load factor

Default load factor is:

0.75

If:

capacity = 16

threshold roughly:

16 × 0.75 = 12

When size crosses the threshold, HashMap resizes.

Usually capacity grows approximately 2×.

16 → 32 → 64 → 128

Resizing is expensive, so if you know approximate size, initialize appropriately.


Why HashMap isn't thread-safe

Two threads can modify the map concurrently.

Use:

java
ConcurrentHashMap

instead of:

java
HashMap

for concurrent access.


Hashtable

`Hashtable` is an older synchronized map implementation.

java
