Compared with HashMap:

| | HashMap | Hashtable |
|---|---|---|
| Thread safe | No | Yes |
| Null key | Allowed | No |
| Null value | Allowed | No |
| Modern usage | Common | Legacy |

For modern concurrent applications, prefer:

java
ConcurrentHashMap

rather than Hashtable.


LinkedHashMap

LinkedHashMap maintains predictable iteration order using an additional linked structure.

Conceptually:

Hash table

bucket
  ↓
nodes

Also:

A ↔ B ↔ C ↔ D

Therefore it can maintain:

Insertion order

It can also be configured for:

Access order

This is useful for implementing an LRU cache.


LinkedList

LinkedList uses nodes:

Node
+-------+
| data  |
| next  |
| prev  |
+-------+

Example:

A ⇄ B ⇄ C ⇄ D

Random access:

java
list.get(5000)

is O(n), unlike ArrayList's O(1) random access.


ArrayList vs LinkedList

| Operation | ArrayList | LinkedList |
|---|---:|---:|
| get(index) | O(1) | O(n) |
| append | O(1) | O(1) |
| insert middle | O(n) | O(n) |
| memory | Lower | Higher |
| cache locality | Better | Worse |

In real applications, ArrayList is often preferable even when LinkedList theoretically has cheaper insertion, because of CPU cache locality and object overhead.


6. Implement Custom LinkedList

java
class CustomLinkedList<T> {

    private Node<T> head;
    private Node<T> tail;
    private int size;

    private static class Node<T> {

        T data;
        Node<T> next;

        Node(T data) {
            this.data = data;
        }
    }

    public void add(T data) {

        Node<T> node = new Node<>(data);

        if (head == null) {
            head = tail = node;
        } else {
            tail.next = node;
            tail = node;
        }

        size++;
    }

    public T get(int index) {

        if (index < 0 || index >= size) {
            throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException();
        }

        Node<T> current = head;

        for (int i = 0; i < index; i++) {
            current = current.next;
        }

        return current.data;
    }

    public int size() {
        return size;
    }
}

Usage:

java
CustomLinkedList<Integer> list =
        new CustomLinkedList<>();

list.add(10);
list.add(20);
list.add(30);

System.out.println(list.get(1));

Output:

20


